    "technique_notes": [
      {
        "heading": "Full-Step Bend with Sustained Vibrato",
        "body": "Every bend in this solo is a full step (two frets in pitch) pushed upward with the ring finger, braced by the middle and index fingers behind it. After the bend reaches the top, immediately roll your wrist \u2014 not your fingers \u2014 in a steady oscillation to add vibrato. The trap is releasing the bend before the vibrato settles in; hold the bent pitch and let the vibrato bloom for at least a beat before you decide to let it go."
      },
      {
        "heading": "D Minor Pentatonic Box at the 10th Position",
        "body": "Gilmour anchors this solo in the D minor pentatonic box starting at fret 10 on the high-e string (D) and reaching up to fret 15 (E\u266d bent to E). Your landmark notes are frets 10 and 13 on string 1 and frets 12 and 13 on string 2 \u2014 internalize those four positions before adding bends. The trap is drifting flat on the 15th fret because the hand hasn't set up early enough; plant the ring finger on 15 before the pick lands, never after."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Pre-Bend Attack",
        "body": "In measure 8 the note at fret 15 is already bent to pitch before it is struck \u2014 a pre-bend. Silently push the string up a full step, THEN pick it, then apply vibrato. If you pick before the bend is fully seated, you hear the pitch glide up, which is a plain bend, not a pre-bend. Check yourself: the struck note should sound like the note at fret 17 without you fretting 17."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Pull-Off Clarity from the 13th to the 10th Fret",
        "body": "The pull-off across a three-fret span (13\u219210) needs the lower finger already planted on fret 10 before you pull. Flick the pulling finger downward toward the floor \u2014 not simply lift it \u2014 so the lower note speaks with volume close to the picked note above it. A lazy pull-off sounds ghostly; aim for both notes at equal volume."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Phrasing Space: Rests Are Not Mistakes",
        "body": "Gilmour's genius in this solo is what he does NOT play. Notice the dotted-quarter bend in measure 4 that is followed by a deliberate eighth-note rest before the phrase continues. Do not rush to fill that silence \u2014 let the bent note ring, release the vibrato, and wait. Players at this level habitually rush past rests; treat each rest as the period at the end of a sentence."
      }
    ],
